WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.662
Learners do not receive new information as a blank slate.

2
00:00:03.662 --> 00:00:11.720
They interpret it through what they already know, what they believe, the vocabulary they recognize, and the examples they have seen before.

3
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.481
Prior knowledge can help because it gives new ideas a place to attach.

4
00:00:16.481 --> 00:00:26.735
It can also get in the way when a learner has a misconception, an incomplete model, or an assumption that works in one context but fails in another.

5
00:00:26.735 --> 00:00:30.764
A mental model is an internal understanding of how something works.

6
00:00:30.764 --> 00:00:35.159
It may be simple, detailed, accurate, incomplete, or wrong in important ways.

7
00:00:35.159 --> 00:00:39.188
Experts often have rich models that let them see patterns quickly.

8
00:00:39.188 --> 00:00:48.344
Beginners may see the same material as a list of isolated facts because they have not yet built the relationships that make the topic coherent.

9
00:00:48.344 --> 00:00:54.936
This is one reason an expert explanation can feel obvious to the expert and confusing to the learner.

10
00:00:54.936 --> 00:00:57.866
Better instruction activates prior knowledge before adding complexity.

11
00:00:57.866 --> 00:01:07.754
That can be as simple as asking what learners already do, naming common assumptions, defining important vocabulary, or using a familiar analogy before introducing the formal concept.

12
00:01:07.754 --> 00:01:10.684
The goal is not to shame weak models.

13
00:01:10.684 --> 00:01:21.671
The goal is to make them visible, test them against examples, and help learners replace them with models that are more useful for the work they actually need to perform.

14
00:01:21.671 --> 00:01:23.869
This is also why vocabulary matters.

15
00:01:23.869 --> 00:01:29.362
A learner who misses one key term may lose the thread of an entire explanation.

16
00:01:29.362 --> 00:01:36.687
Good courses define terms when they first matter, revisit them in context, and show how the terms relate to actions.

17
00:01:36.687 --> 00:01:39.251
The learner is not just collecting definitions.

18
00:01:39.251 --> 00:01:43.280
They are building a map that helps them navigate the subject.
